The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
raise_softirq_irqoff(RCU_SOFTIRQ);
```
Reason:  
This call in `rcu_read_unlock_special()` is made without any guard against re-entry. When a trace event (e.g., softirq entry tracing) is triggered by `raise_softirq_irqoff()`, it can lead to a nested `rcu_read_unlock_special()` invocation. Without the previous recursion protection (removed by commit 5f5fa7ea89dc), the nested call again executes `raise_softirq_irqoff()` unconditionally, causing an infinite loop.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (rdp->defer_qs_pending != DEFER_QS_PENDING) {
	rdp->defer_qs_pending = DEFER_QS_PENDING;
	raise_softirq_irqoff(RCU_SOFTIRQ);
}
```
Reason:  
A pending flag (`defer_qs_pending`) is introduced to prevent re-entering `raise_softirq_irqoff()` when a softirq raise is already in progress. By setting the flag before the call, subsequent nested returns will see the flag already set and skip the call, breaking the deadloop. The flag is cleared after the softirq is handled, allowing future raises.

